This code demonstrates a critical template injection vulnerability by accepting unsanitized user input directly from the query string parameter `name` and interpolating it into a Jinja2 template string using `.format()`. The user-supplied `name` value is passed directly to `render_template_string()` with no validation or escaping, allowing an attacker to inject arbitrary Jinja2 template syntax such as `{{7*7}}` or `{{config}}` into the HTML response. This enables Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) attacks where an attacker can execute arbitrary code in the application, read sensitive configuration data, traverse the filesystem, or potentially achieve remote code execution depending on the Flask application's privileges. An attacker could craft a malicious URL like `?name={{self.__class__.__globals__.__builtins__.__import__('os').system('calc')}}` to execute system commands on the server. The vulnerability persists because the code uses string formatting to build the template and never sanitizes or escapes the user input before passing it to Jinja2, failing to separate template code from user data.

